    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '51. A 72-year-old with chronic HFrEF presents with acute dyspnea, bilateral crackles, JVD, and lower extremity edema. SpO2 is 88%. What is the most appropriate initial intervention?', '["IV furosemide and non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV)", "Emergent intubation", "IV normal saline bolus", "Oral metoprolol"]', 0, 'Acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF) is managed with IV loop diuretics (furosemide) for volume overload and NIPPV (BiPAP/CPAP) to improve oxygenation and reduce preload/afterload.'),
    (tid, '52. Which of the following is the most useful bedside test to differentiate cardiac from pulmonary causes of acute dyspnea?', '["Chest X-ray", "BNP or NT-proBNP", "D-dimer", "Peak expiratory flow rate"]', 1, 'BNP >400 pg/mL or NT-proBNP >900 pg/mL strongly suggests heart failure as the cause of dyspnea. Values <100 (BNP) or <300 (NT-proBNP) effectively rule out HF.'),
    (tid, '53. A patient with acute pulmonary edema has BP 190/110. Which vasodilator is most appropriate for rapid afterload reduction?', '["IV nitroglycerin infusion", "IV hydralazine", "Oral captopril", "IV labetalol"]', 0, 'IV nitroglycerin is preferred in hypertensive acute pulmonary edema because it rapidly reduces preload (venodilation) and afterload, decreasing pulmonary congestion within minutes. Start at 5-10 mcg/min.'),
    (tid, '54. In cardiogenic shock, which hemodynamic profile is expected on pulmonary artery catheterization?', '["Low PCWP, low CO, low SVR", "High PCWP, low CO, high SVR", "Low PCWP, high CO, low SVR", "High PCWP, high CO, low SVR"]', 1, 'Cardiogenic shock = pump failure. High PCWP (fluid backs up), low CO (pump fails), high SVR (compensatory vasoconstriction). This distinguishes it from septic shock (low SVR) and hypovolemic shock (low PCWP).'),
    (tid, '55. Which inotrope is preferred in cardiogenic shock when the patient is also hypotensive (SBP <90)?', '["Dobutamine", "Milrinone", "Norepinephrine", "Dopamine at high dose or norepinephrine + dobutamine"]', 3, 'Isolated dobutamine can worsen hypotension via beta-2 vasodilation. In cardiogenic shock with SBP <90, a vasopressor (norepinephrine) is needed first to maintain perfusion pressure, then dobutamine is added for inotropy.'),
    (tid, '56. A patient with acute MI develops sudden hemodynamic collapse with muffled heart sounds, JVD, and hypotension. Pulsus paradoxus is 18 mmHg. What is the diagnosis?', '["Tension pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade from free wall rupture", "Massive PE", "Aortic dissection"]', 1, 'Beck triad (muffled hearts sounds, JVD, hypotension) + pulsus paradoxus >10 mmHg = cardiac tamponade. Free wall rupture is a catastrophic mechanical complication occurring 3-5 days post-MI.'),
    (tid, '57. What is the definitive emergency treatment for cardiac tamponade?', '["IV fluid resuscitation", "Pericardiocentesis", "Chest tube placement", "Emergency thoracotomy"]', 1, 'Pericardiocentesis (needle aspiration of pericardial fluid) is the definitive emergency treatment. Even removing 20-30 mL can dramatically improve hemodynamics. Echocardiography guidance improves safety.'),
    (tid, '58. A 28-year-old IV drug user presents with fever, a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration, and splinter hemorrhages. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Mitral valve prolapse", "Tricuspid valve endocarditis", "Aortic stenosis", "Rheumatic mitral stenosis"]', 1, 'IV drug use predisposes to right-sided (tricuspid) endocarditis. The murmur increases with inspiration (Carvallo sign). Splinter hemorrhages are a peripheral sign of endocarditis. S. aureus is the most common pathogen.'),
    (tid, '59. A patient with known aortic stenosis presents with syncope during exertion. This is considered a high-risk feature because syncope in AS indicates what?', '["Dehydration", "Critical outflow obstruction with fixed cardiac output", "Autonomic dysfunction", "Carotid artery disease"]', 1, 'In severe AS, cardiac output is fixed by the stenotic valve. During exertion, peripheral vasodilation occurs but CO cannot increase, causing a drop in cerebral perfusion and syncope. This portends sudden death.'),
    (tid, '60. Which valvular emergency can present with acute flash pulmonary edema after an MI?', '["Aortic stenosis", "Mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture", "Tricuspid regurgitation", "Pulmonic stenosis"]', 1, 'Acute severe MR from papillary muscle rupture (typically posteromedial papillary muscle, supplied by PDA) causes sudden volume overload of the LA and flash pulmonary edema 2-7 days post-MI.'),
    (tid, '61. What is the classic ECG finding in severe hyperkalemia?', '["Shortened QT interval", "Peaked T waves progressing to sine wave pattern", "ST elevation in V1-V4", "Delta waves"]', 1, 'Hyperkalemia ECG progression: peaked T waves (5.5-6.5) → PR prolongation and P wave flattening (6.5-7.5) → QRS widening (7.0-8.0) → sine wave pattern → VF/asystole (>8.0 mEq/L).'),
    (tid, '62. A patient with K+ of 7.2 mEq/L has widened QRS complexes on ECG. What is the FIRST medication to administer?', '["IV insulin + dextrose", "IV calcium gluconate", "Sodium bicarbonate", "Kayexalate"]', 1, 'IV calcium gluconate (or chloride) is given FIRST because it stabilizes the myocardial membrane within 1-3 minutes, preventing lethal arrhythmias. It does NOT lower K+ but buys time for other treatments.'),
    (tid, '63. A 55-year-old with HFpEF (preserved EF) presents with dyspnea on exertion. Echo shows EF 60%, diastolic dysfunction grade II, and elevated E/e ratio. What is the primary pathophysiology?', '["Systolic dysfunction", "Impaired ventricular relaxation and filling", "Valvular regurgitation", "Pericardial constriction"]', 1, 'HFpEF is caused by diastolic dysfunction — impaired ventricular relaxation and increased stiffness leads to elevated filling pressures despite normal EF. Common in elderly, hypertensive, and diabetic patients.'),
    (tid, '64. Which medication class has been shown to reduce mortality in HFrEF?', '["Calcium channel blockers", "ACE inhibitors / ARBs", "Digoxin", "NSAIDs"]', 1, 'ACE inhibitors (and ARBs if ACE-I intolerant) reduce mortality in HFrEF by blocking the RAAS, reducing afterload, preventing remodeling, and decreasing aldosterone-mediated fluid retention.'),
    (tid, '65. A patient on an ACE inhibitor for heart failure develops a dry persistent cough. What is the recommended alternative?', '["Increase ACE inhibitor dose", "Switch to an ARB", "Add a cough suppressant", "Discontinue all RAAS inhibition"]', 1, 'ACE-I cough is caused by bradykinin accumulation. ARBs (e.g., losartan, valsartan) block angiotensin II at the receptor level without affecting bradykinin metabolism and are the recommended substitute.'),
    (tid, '66. In acute decompensated heart failure, when should IV nitroglycerin be AVOIDED?', '["SBP >180 mmHg", "SBP <90 mmHg or recent use of PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil)", "Heart rate >100 bpm", "Bilateral pleural effusions"]', 1, 'Nitrates are contraindicated in hypotension (SBP <90) and within 24-48 hours of PDE5 inhibitor use (sildenafil/tadalafil) due to risk of severe refractory hypotension from synergistic vasodilation.'),
    (tid, '67. A patient with decompensated HF has serum sodium of 125 mEq/L. What does hyponatremia indicate in this context?', '["Dehydration", "Poor prognosis due to neurohormonal activation and fluid retention", "Renal failure", "Adrenal insufficiency"]', 1, 'Hyponatremia in HF reflects excessive ADH secretion and severe neurohormonal activation (RAAS, sympathetic). It is an independent predictor of mortality and indicates advanced disease.'),
    (tid, '68. Which mechanical circulatory support device is used as a bridge to recovery or transplant in refractory cardiogenic shock?', '["Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP)", "Impella or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO)", "Temporary pacemaker", "Chest compressions device (LUCAS)"]', 1, 'Impella (percutaneous ventricular assist) and VA-ECMO provide superior hemodynamic support compared to IABP in refractory cardiogenic shock. IABP has fallen out of favor based on IABP-SHOCK II trial.'),
    (tid, '69. What is the mechanism of action of the intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP)?', '["Increases afterload during systole", "Inflates during diastole to augment coronary perfusion and deflates during systole to reduce afterload", "Provides continuous flow like a VAD", "Acts as an external pacemaker"]', 1, 'IABP uses counterpulsation: inflation during diastole increases coronary and cerebral perfusion; deflation just before systole reduces afterload (afterload reduction), decreasing myocardial oxygen demand.'),
    (tid, '70. A 40-year-old presents with acute onset severe dyspnea, hypotension, and a new loud S3 gallop. Echo shows global hypokinesis with EF 15%. Coronary angiography is normal. Likely diagnosis?', '["Acute MI", "Acute myocarditis", "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy", "Constrictive pericarditis"]', 1, 'Acute myocarditis presents with acute HF symptoms, global LV dysfunction, and normal coronaries. Often preceded by viral illness. Coxsackievirus B is the classic cause. Diagnosis confirmed by cardiac MRI or biopsy.'),
    (tid, '71. A postpartum woman (5 months after delivery) presents with progressive dyspnea and bilateral leg edema. Echo shows dilated LV with EF 25%. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pulmonary embolism", "Peripartum cardiomyopathy", "Pre-eclampsia", "Amniotic fluid embolism"]', 1, 'Peripartum cardiomyopathy presents as new-onset HF between the last month of pregnancy and 5 months postpartum in the absence of other identifiable causes. EF is typically <45%.'),
    (tid, '72. Which finding on chest X-ray is most specific for acute pulmonary edema?', '["Kerley B lines", "Bilateral perihilar alveolar opacities (butterfly pattern)", "Pleural effusions", "Cardiomegaly"]', 1, 'Bilateral perihilar bat-wing or butterfly opacities are the most characteristic CXR finding of acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Other findings include cephalization, Kerley B lines, and peribronchial cuffing.'),
    (tid, '73. A patient with severe mitral stenosis and atrial fibrillation presents with sudden left leg pain and absent pulses. What is the most likely cause?', '["Deep vein thrombosis", "Arterial embolism from left atrial thrombus", "Aortic dissection", "Peripheral artery disease"]', 1, 'Mitral stenosis causes left atrial dilation, stasis, and thrombus formation, especially in AF. Embolization to the peripheral arteries (most commonly the legs) causes acute limb ischemia.'),
    (tid, '74. What is the most common cause of acute right heart failure in the emergency setting?', '["Right ventricular MI", "Acute massive pulmonary embolism", "Tricuspid endocarditis", "Pulmonary hypertension"]', 1, 'Massive PE causes acute RV pressure overload and failure because the RV cannot generate enough force to overcome the sudden increase in pulmonary vascular resistance.'),
    (tid, '75. In a patient with acute right heart failure from massive PE, which fluid strategy is correct?', '["Aggressive IV fluids (2-3 liters)", "Cautious fluid challenge (250-500 mL) with reassessment", "Strict fluid restriction", "IV furosemide immediately"]', 1, 'The failing RV is preload-sensitive but volume-intolerant. A cautious 250-500 mL bolus may improve CO, but excessive fluid worsens RV dilation, shifts the septum leftward (D-sign), and impairs LV filling.');
